{"id":"assembling-objects","title":"Assembling Objects","sub":"Spatial reasoning: where connections go and how pieces fit together","label":"","file":"15-ao.md","html":"<p>Assembling Objects (AO) measures <strong>spatial ability</strong> — how well you can picture how parts fit together. Every question is a picture. On the computer test you get <strong>15 questions in 18 minutes</strong> (about 1 minute 12 seconds each); the paper test has 25 questions in 15 minutes.</p>\n<p>AO is <strong>not</strong> part of the AFQT, and it is not given on the student (high school) ASVAB. It is used mainly in some <strong>Navy</strong> job composites. Still, it is worth a little practice: the questions follow a few simple patterns, and careful checking turns them into easy points.</p>\n<h2>The Two Kinds of AO Questions</h2>\n<h3>1. Connection questions</h3>\n<p>The question box shows two shapes. Each shape has a point marked with a letter — <strong>A</strong> on one shape and <strong>B</strong> on the other. Your job is to find the answer choice in which a line connects <strong>exactly those two points</strong>.</p>\n<figure class=\"fig\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-label=\"figure\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 680 300\" width=\"680\" height=\"300\" font-family=\"Segoe UI, Arial, sans-serif\"><svg x=\"190\" y=\"4\" width=\"300\" height=\"130\" viewBox=\"0 0 300 130\"><rect x=\"1\" y=\"1\" width=\"298\" height=\"128\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#9aa3ae\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/><polygon points=\"80,99.5 109.88,47.75 50.12,47.75\" fill=\"#eef1f5\" stroke=\"#23272e\" stroke-width=\"1.8\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/><circle cx=\"80\" cy=\"99.5\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#c93b3b\" stroke=\"#c93b3b\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><text x=\"80\" y=\"114.5\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#c93b3b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"800\" dominant-baseline=\"middle\">A</text><polygon points=\"250,65 249.74,61.08 248.98,57.24 247.72,53.52 245.98,50 243.8,46.74 241.21,43.79 238.26,41.2 235,39.